Field-mode audio fixtures — attribution and processing
=====================================================

All recordings come from xeno-canto (https://xeno-canto.org) and are redistributed here under
their original Creative Commons licences. Each WAV is a trimmed, resampled, level-normalized
excerpt of the original; under CC BY-SA / BY-NC-SA these excerpts are adaptations and are
shared under the same licence as their source. No ND-licensed recording was used.
These WAV files are NOT covered by the repository's GPLv3 licence: each is licensed only
under the CC licence in its row below, and is distributed alongside (not as part of) the
GPL code as separate test data. Copyright in each recording remains with its recordist.
Licences, recordists and species were re-checked against each xeno-canto page on 2026-11-22.
Machine-readable copy of this table: manifest.toml (same directory).

Common processing (scripts/build_field_audio_fixtures.py reproduces every file):
  - excerpt [source start, source start + duration) from the original upload;
  - one source channel kept (channel 0; channels never summed — spaced stereo pairs can be
    anti-correlated, which would comb-filter a mix; XC795439 and XC980972 are stereo sources);
  - DC removed; resampled to 48 kHz with scipy.signal.resample_poly (polyphase, Kaiser FIR);
  - 10 ms raised-cosine fade in/out at both edges;
  - bird clips scaled to a -3 dBFS sample peak; the ambience clip scaled to -30 dBFS RMS;
  - written as 48 kHz mono 16-bit PCM WAV (round-to-nearest, no dither).
  No filtering, denoising or EQ was applied beyond what the recordist did (noted below).

